WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.389
Anne was sitting with Ruby Gillis in the Gillis' garden watching the sunset.

2
00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.828
Over the summer Anne had spent many pleasant evenings with Ruby.

3
00:00:10.829 --> 00:00:17.678
Anne had watched Ruby grow paler and weaker, and each evening ended a little earlier than the last.

4
00:00:17.679 --> 00:00:27.833
But Ruby was always cheerful and talkative, always chatting about young men and clothes and the latest Avonlea news, and never mentioned her health.

5
00:00:27.834 --> 00:00:32.469
At the end of every visit, Ruby made Anne promise to visit again.

6
00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:37.637
Once, Mrs. Lynde complained about Anne's frequent visits to the Gillis' house.

7
00:00:37.638 --> 00:00:43.702
"Every time you go and visit Ruby, you come back looking so tired, Anne," she said.

8
00:00:43.703 --> 00:00:50.116
"It's so sad," Anne said. "Ruby doesn't seem to realize that she's sick.

9
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:55.005
I feel as if I'm watching her fight an invisible enemy."

10
00:00:55.006 --> 00:01:01.299
But when Anne went to visit Ruby one evening at the end of summer, Ruby was strangely quiet.

11
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.019
She did not say a word about parties or new clothes or young men.

12
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:11.706
Ruby sat in her chair on the porch, with a white shawl wrapped around her thin shoulders.

13
00:01:11.707 --> 00:01:16.647
Just beyond the Gillis' house was the church, with the old graveyard beside it.

14
00:01:16.648 --> 00:01:22.057
The moonlight shone on the white stones.

15
00:01:22.058 --> 00:01:26.450
"How strange the graveyard looks by moonlight," said Ruby suddenly.

16
00:01:26.451 --> 00:01:33.360
"How ghostly." She shuddered. "Anne, it won't be long now before I'll be lying over there.

17
00:01:33.361 --> 00:01:39.406
You and Diana and everyone else will keep on living . . . and I'll be there . . . in the old graveyard—dead!"

18
00:01:39.407 --> 00:01:44.137
Anne was shocked. For a few moments, she could not speak.

19
00:01:44.138 --> 00:01:48.414
"You know it's true, don't you?" said Ruby insistently.

20
00:01:48.415 --> 00:01:52.565
"Yes, I know," answered Anne in a low tone.

21
00:01:52.566 --> 00:02:00.303
"Everybody knows it," said Ruby bitterly. "I know it. I've known all summer, but I didn't want to give up.

22
00:02:00.304 --> 00:02:07.401
Oh, Anne!" Ruby reached out and grabbed Anne's hand pleadingly. "I'm afraid to die."

23
00:02:07.402 --> 00:02:11.906
"Why are you afraid, Ruby?" asked Anne quietly.

24
00:02:11.907 --> 00:02:18.315
"Well," Ruby replied, "I believe there is a heaven, and I think heaven must be very beautiful, of course . . .

25
00:02:18.316 --> 00:02:21.293
but Anne, it won't be what I'm used to."

26
00:02:21.294 --> 00:02:26.456
Anne looked at Ruby and said, "I think heaven will be similar to life here.

27
00:02:26.457 --> 00:02:32.930
In heaven it will just be easier to be good. Don't be afraid, Ruby."

28
00:02:32.931 --> 00:02:36.866
"I can't help it," said Ruby pitifully.

29
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:40.730
"Even if what you say about heaven is true, it won't be the same.

30
00:02:40.731 --> 00:02:46.453
It can't be. I want to go on living here . I'm so young,

31
00:02:46.454 --> 00:02:49.634
Anne. I've fought so hard to live and it isn't any

32
00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:53.998
use. I have to die—and leave everything I care

33
00:02:53.999 --> 00:02:58.700
for." Anne sat still. The silence was almost

34
00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:04.296
intolerable. Anne could not tell Ruby comforting lies; everything that Ruby said was horribly

35
00:03:04.297 --> 00:03:07.868
true. She was leaving everything that she cared

36
00:03:07.869 --> 00:03:11.992
for. "I want to live," Ruby repeated in a trembling

37
00:03:11.993 --> 00:03:20.703
voice. "I want to live like other girls. I . . . I want to be married, and . . . have

38
00:03:20.704 --> 00:03:26.301
babies. Anne, I couldn't say this to anyone but you. I know you understand.

39
00:03:26.302 --> 00:03:31.429
And then poor Tony Spencer . . . He loves me and I love him, Anne.

40
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.474
The others meant nothing to me, but he does, and if I could live, I would be his wife.

41
00:03:36.475 --> 00:03:42.761
I would be so happy. Oh, Anne!" Ruby sobbed.

42
00:03:42.762 --> 00:03:46.584
Anne held Ruby's hand, and offered her silent sympathy.

43
00:03:46.585 --> 00:03:52.929
It was a relief to Anne not to have to use clumsy words, and her silence was a deeper comfort to Ruby.

44
00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.679
Gradually Ruby stopped crying and became calm.

45
00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.532
"I'm glad I've told you this, Anne," she whispered.

46
00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:03.306
"It has helped me just to speak about it.

47
00:04:03.307 --> 00:04:07.401
I've wanted to talk it over with you for a long time, but I couldn't.

48
00:04:07.402 --> 00:04:10.553
It would have made death seem real.

49
00:04:10.554 --> 00:04:16.935
In the daytime, when people were around me and everything was cheerful, it wasn't that hard to stop thinking about it.

50
00:04:16.936 --> 00:04:21.024
But at nighttime, when I couldn't sleep, it was so dreadful, Anne.

51
00:04:21.025 --> 00:04:25.506
I would just lie in bed and think about death and get so scared."

52
00:04:25.507 --> 00:04:32.143
Anne said, "Don't be frightened anymore, Ruby. Just believe everything will be okay."

53
00:04:32.144 --> 00:04:36.610
"I'll try," Ruby replied. "I try to be brave.

54
00:04:36.611 --> 00:04:39.456
Please come and see me as often as you can."

55
00:04:39.457 --> 00:04:42.630
"I will," Anne replied.

56
00:04:42.631 --> 00:04:45.734
"It . . . it won't be very long now, Anne.

57
00:04:45.735 --> 00:04:51.770
I feel sure of that," said Ruby, "and I'd rather spend time with you than anyone else."

58
00:04:51.771 --> 00:04:54.838
"I must go now, Ruby," Anne said.

59
00:04:54.839 --> 00:04:58.209
"It's getting late, and you shouldn't be out in the cold."

60
00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.919
"Goodnight, Anne," said Ruby.

61
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.206
Anne walked home very slowly in the moonlight.

62
00:05:04.207 --> 00:05:07.164
The evening had changed something for her.

63
00:05:07.165 --> 00:05:10.820
Life held a different meaning, a deeper purpose.

64
00:05:10.821 --> 00:05:19.321
It seemed more important to her than ever to live life to the fullest, to love, and not to be afraid of the challenges that might lie ahead.

65
00:05:19.322 --> 00:05:27.706
Anne never saw Ruby again. The next morning the news went from house to house that Ruby Gillis was dead.

66
00:05:27.707 --> 00:05:31.738
She had died in her sleep, painlessly and calmly.

67
00:05:31.739 --> 00:05:40.083
On her face was a smile, as if death had come as a kindly friend instead of the phantom Ruby had dreaded.

68
00:05:40.084 --> 00:05:45.995
After the funeral Mrs. Gillis called Anne aside and gave her a small packet.

69
00:05:45.996 --> 00:05:49.730
"I want you to have this," she sobbed.

70
00:05:49.731 --> 00:05:52.633
"Ruby would have liked you to have it.

71
00:05:52.634 --> 00:05:59.553
It's some embroidery she was doing the afternoon before she died. It isn't quite finished."

72
00:05:59.554 --> 00:06:09.016
Mrs. Lynde was standing with Mrs. Gillis. "There's always a piece of unfinished work left," she said with tears in her eyes.

73
00:06:09.017 --> 00:06:12.767
"But I suppose there's always someone to finish it."

74
00:06:12.768 --> 00:06:19.136
Diana and the residents of Green Gables quietly walked home from Ruby's funeral.

75
00:06:19.137 --> 00:06:23.561
Davy said suddenly, "Ruby Gillis had a great laugh.

76
00:06:23.562 --> 00:06:28.887
Will she laugh as much in heaven as she did in Avonlea, Anne? I want to know."

77
00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:39.171
"Yes, I think she will," said Anne.

